-- title:
--   Admissible jump operator: single-qubit Pauli times a syndrome function
-- statement:
--   A jump operator $S$ on the qubits of a CSS code is **admissible** if
--
--   $$S=P\cdot\sum_{\sigma_Z,\sigma_X}f(\sigma_Z,\sigma_X)\,\Pi_{\sigma_Z}\Pi_{\sigma_X}$$
--
--   for some single-qubit Pauli $P\in\{X_q,Y_q,Z_q\}$ and some complex function $f$ of the syndrome. The second factor is an arbitrary element of the algebra generated by the stabilizers; $f\equiv1$ gives the bare single-qubit Paulis.
--
--   These are the jump operators of the thermalizing dynamics of §2.5 ("jump operators in the set $\mathcal E$", single-site Pauli errors, footnote 7). Davies generators with single-qubit Pauli coupling have jump operators of this form, and their Lindbladians have the Gibbs state as a fixed point.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Bare single-qubit Pauli jumps alone generate a unital Lindbladian, which can have a non-maximally-mixed Gibbs state as a fixed point only if it vanishes; the class $P\cdot F$ contains them and the energy-resolved Davies jumps.
-- source:
--   Balasubramanian, Davydova, Lin, A passive self-correcting quantum memory in three dimensions, arXiv:2605.10943, §2.5, pp. 19–21 and footnote 7; p. 4

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_SelfCorrect3D_Memory_singleQubitPaulis
import Definitions.Def_SelfCorrect3D_Memory_CSSCode_syndProjX
import Definitions.Def_SelfCorrect3D_Memory_CSSCode_syndProjZ

namespace SelfCorrect3D.Memory

/-- **Admissible jump operator**: a single-qubit Pauli times a function of the syndrome,
`S = P · Σ_{σ_Z, σ_X} f(σ_Z, σ_X) Π_{σ_Z} Π_{σ_X}` with `P ∈ {X_q, Y_q, Z_q}`.
Balasubramanian, Davydova, Lin, *A passive self-correcting quantum memory in three dimensions*, arXiv:2605.10943, §2.5, pp. 19–21 (jump operators "in the set 𝓔", footnote 7:
"single-site Pauli jump operators") and p. 4 ("generated by local jump operators").

**Formalization Note (reading).** The second factor ranges over the whole algebra generated by
the stabilizers (every element is such a syndrome function); `f ≡ 1` gives the bare Paulis. Jump
operators that are literally single-qubit Paulis generate a unital Lindbladian, whose only
stationary Gibbs state is at `β = 0` unless `𝓛 = 0`; the class `P · F` contains these and the
jump operators of Davies generators (Pauli coupling, energy-resolved), which do have `ρ_β` as a
fixed point. -/
def IsAdmissibleJump {n mX mZ : ℕ} (C : CSSCode n mX mZ) (S : Op n) : Prop :=
  ∃ P ∈ singleQubitPaulis n, ∃ f : (Fin mX → ZMod 2) → (Fin mZ → ZMod 2) → ℂ,
    S = P * ∑ σZ, ∑ σX, f σZ σX • (C.syndProjX σZ * C.syndProjZ σX)

end SelfCorrect3D.Memory


